      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which panel height makes a pool fence in Florida?",
            "answer": "<p>Florida's Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act sets a barrier at least 48 inches high on the outside. Gates must close on their own and open away from the pool. The 46-inch tempered panel in two spigots gives a 48-inch railing, so it is the panel to order for a pool. The 57.7-inch panel gives 60 inches if you want more. Confirm the details with your local building department before ordering.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a permit for a glass railing in Florida?",
            "answer": "<p>Permits are issued by the city or county building department, not by the state, so the answer depends on where you live. A guard on a deck, balcony or pool is life-safety work, so ask the building department before ordering rather than after. Plan reviewers ask how the spigots attach. Our <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-engineering-testing\">engineering and testing page</a> has the testing and blocking details for both spigots on concrete and on wood.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does salt air on the Florida coast affect the hardware?",
            "answer": "<p>Salt air is the reason we use 2205 marine-grade stainless steel for every spigot, connector, hinge and latch. Both finishes, Brushed Stainless and Matte Black, carry a 10-year no-rust promise. On oceanfront and Intracoastal lots, rinse the hardware with fresh water now and then to wash off salt deposits. The tempered glass itself is unaffected by salt, and a regular glass cleaner keeps it clear.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "When should I order to have the railing in before hurricane season?",
            "answer": "<p>Hurricane season runs from June through November. Standard panels are processed in 2 to 3 business days and delivered in 5 to 10 business days. A standard order placed in April is normally on site in May. Custom sizes add 2 to 3 weeks of shipping. Most standard installations take about 4 hours, which leaves time to finish the job before the summer storms arrive.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        }
      },
