        "h": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Frameless glass railings for Florida pools, balconies and waterfront decks"
          }
        },
        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Where Florida installs glass railings</strong></p><p>South Florida is condo country. Balconies in Miami, Fort Lauderdale, Boca Raton and West Palm Beach face the ocean or the Intracoastal. A frameless glass balcony railing keeps that view open. Miami-Dade and Broward sit in the High-Velocity Hurricane Zone, so building departments there look closely at how a railing attaches. The Gulf Coast is different: canal homes in Cape Coral and Fort Myers, pool decks in Naples and Sarasota, bay-front lots in Tampa and St. Petersburg. Salt air is the common factor on both coasts.</p><p>Central Florida is pool-home country. Orlando, Windermere and Lake Nona houses have screened lanais, and a glass pool fence keeps the yard open. Jacksonville and St. Augustine add riverfront decks on the St. Johns and beach houses at Ponte Vedra. In the Panhandle, Destin, 30A and Pensacola Beach homes stand on pilings, with tall stairs and second-floor porches that need a guard.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>The Florida code in short</strong></p><p>Florida uses one statewide code, the Florida Building Code, 8th edition (2023). For a house, the residential volume sets a 36-inch minimum guard on a deck, balcony or porch more than 30 inches above grade. Commercial and multifamily buildings, which include condos, fall under the building volume and need 42 inches. Our 33.6-inch panel gives a 36-inch railing and the 39.6-inch panel gives 42 inches.</p><p>Pools follow the Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act (F.S. 515.29). The barrier must be at least 48 inches high on the outside. Gates must close on their own and open away from the pool. The 46-inch panel reaches 48 inches, and the 57.7-inch panel makes a 60-inch fence. Miami-Dade and Broward are the HVHZ, and every coastal county has high design wind speeds, so the local building department reviews railing attachments. Read our <a href=\"/pages/florida-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Florida building code page</a>, then confirm with your building department.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery anywhere in Florida</strong></p><p>Every order ships from our warehouse in Sainte-Julie, Quebec. Standard panels are processed in 2 to 3 business days, crated, and delivered in 5 to 10 business days by freight carrier. The truck has a liftgate, so the crate comes down to the driveway or the loading dock. Custom sizes take 2 to 3 business days to process and then 2 to 3 weeks in transit. Orders over 3,000 dollars ship free. The checkout price is final: no duties, tariffs or hidden fees, even though the glass crosses the border.</p><p>Check the crate on arrival. Damage reported within 3 days of delivery, and before installation, is replaced or refunded at no cost. From there, the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-installation-guide\">installation guide</a> and videos walk you through a standard job in about 4 hours. Spigots fix to wood or concrete, and the screws are sold separately.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
