    "cols": {
      "type": "grs-pg-cols",
      "blocks": {
        "c1": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Glass pool fences",
            "text": "<p>Florida's pool barrier rule is 48 inches, and the 46-inch panel in two spigots lands right there. Add gate hinges, a latch and a floor lock, and the fence stays clear glass from the lanai to the yard. The 57.7-inch panel gives 60 inches when you want more height.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Condo balcony railings",
            "text": "<p>From Brickell to Singer Island, condo balconies are concrete slabs with a 42-inch guard rule under the building volume. Top-mount spigots anchor into the slab, or side-mount spigots fix to the edge and keep the floor clear. Associations approve exterior changes, so share the engineering documents first.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Waterfront decks and beach stairs",
            "text": "<p>Canal homes in Cape Coral, bay lots on Davis Islands and piling houses on 30A all put decks and stairs in salt air. The 2205 stainless spigots carry a 10-year no-rust promise, and 12 mm tempered glass does not block the water view. Stair runs use custom-cut panels.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
